The officers at the Cornwall office are: 1 division engineer; I assistant engineer; 1 junior engineer; 2 instrument men; 2 rodmen; 2 engineering helpers; 1 stenographer grade 1; 6 gauge readers; 2 labourers. The total including travelling ex-
Supply-Railways and Canals .

penses is 327,510. At the head office the officers are: 1 senior office engineer; 1 office engineer; 2 assistant engineers; 1 electrical power house engineer; 1 principal draftsman; 1 stenographer, grade 3; 1 assistant engineer;
1	stenographer, grade 2; 1 senior draftsman;
2	designing engineers; 1 mechanical and electrical draftsman; 2 extra draftsmen from Cornwall. The total including expenses, stationery, and so forth, is $40,360. In charge of the engineering board-I think my hon. friend is interested in this particularly-there are two board members at 15 days per month, 12 months of 360 days at $50 or $18,000.

I do. I am not quite sure that the harbour commission to which my hon. friend refers is on the same footing as the Vancouver Harbour Commission or this commission. My hon. friend will find that under subsection (d) of section 11 the commissioners may by by-law provide for:
The compensation or salaries to be paid the officers, assistants, engineers, clerks and servants appointed by the corporation;

And further on he will see that the bylaws will have no force or effect until they are confirmed by the Governor in Council. So the salary by-laws will have to be approved by order in council. I am not sure that there is a similar provision in other cases.
